{"causes": ["The establishment of the Mạc dynasty by his grandfather Mạc Đăng Dung in 1527 created a new imperial lineage in Đại Việt.", "His father, Mạc Thái Tông, died in 1540, necessitating his succession as the eldest son to the Mạc imperial throne.", "The ongoing civil war between the Mạc dynasty and the Restored Lê dynasty, supported by the Trịnh and Nguyễn clans, defined the political landscape he inherited."], "effects": ["His reign ensured the immediate continuity of the Mạc dynasty, maintaining its control over northern Đại Việt for another six years.", "The Mạc-Lê civil war continued unabated, further entrenching the division of Đại Việt into northern and southern spheres of influence.", "His death in 1546 led to the succession of Mạc Tuyên Tông, ushering in a period of increased internal instability within the Mạc court."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "mc-thi-tng", "sourceName": "Mạc Thái Tông", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "mc-hin-tng", "targetName": "Mạc Hiến Tông", "context": "Mạc Thái Tông's death in 1540 directly led to Mạc Hiến Tông's ascension to the Mạc imperial throne."}, {"sourceSlug": "mc-hin-tng", "sourceName": "Mạc Hiến Tông", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "mc-l-civil-war", "targetName": "Mạc-Lê Civil War", "context": "Mạc Hiến Tông actively participated in the Mạc-Lê Civil War as the Mạc emperor from 1540 to 1546, defending his dynasty's control over Đại Việt."}, {"sourceSlug": "mc-hin-tng", "sourceName": "Mạc Hiến Tông", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "thng-long-i-vit", "targetName": "Thăng Long, Đại Việt", "context": "Mạc Hiến Tông's reign was centered in Thăng Long, the Mạc dynasty's capital in northern Đại Việt, from 1540 to 1546."}, {"sourceSlug": "restored-le-dynasty", "sourceName": "Restored Lê Dynasty", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "mc-hin-tng", "targetName": "Mạc Hiến Tông", "context": "The military and political pressure from the Restored Lê dynasty significantly influenced Mạc Hiến Tông's strategic decisions and focus on defense during his reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "mc-hin-tng", "sourceName": "Mạc Hiến Tông", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "mc-tuyn-tng", "targetName": "Mạc Tuyên Tông", "context": "Mạc Hiến Tông's death in 1546 directly led to the succession of his younger brother, Mạc Tuyên Tông, as the next Mạc emperor."}], "places": [{"name": "Cao Đôi village, Bình Hà district, Đại Việt", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Thăng Long, Đại Việt", "role": "Imperial Capital"}, {"name": "Annam", "role": "Region of Rule"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Mạc Hiến Tông", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "mạc-hiến-tông"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q985676", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%E1%BA%A1c_Hi%E1%BA%BFn_T%C3%B4ng"], "tags": ["Vietnam", "Monarchs & Rulers", "Asia", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Mạc Hiến Tông is remembered as a landmark person of the Classical era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries; Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati. Their legacy is studied through the lens of political thought and imperial studies.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Mạc Hiến Tông's reign was significant for temporarily stabilizing the Mạc dynasty's hold on northern Đại Việt during a critical phase of the Mạc-Lê Civil War, thus preventing an immediate collapse and prolonging the dynastic conflict for decades. His efforts contributed to the long-term political fragmentation of Vietnam.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-06T23:16:54Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42309720987", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Mạc Hiến Tông, birth name Mạc Phúc Hải, was the third emperor of the Mạc dynasty of Annam from 1540 to 1546. He was born in Cao Đôi village, Bình Hà district. He was the oldest son of emperor Mac Thai Tong and grandson of Mac Dang Dung.", "newValue": "Mạc Hiến Tông, born Mạc Phúc Hải, ascended to the imperial throne of the Mạc dynasty of Đại Việt (Annam) in 1540, reigning until his death on June 16, 1546. As the third emperor of the Mạc, he inherited a realm embroiled in a protracted civil war with the resurgent Lê dynasty, supported by the powerful Trịnh and Nguyễn clans. His brief reign was characterized by efforts to consolidate Mạc authority in the northern territories while facing relentless military pressure from the south, marking a critical period in the Mạc dynasty's struggle for legitimacy and survival against the backdrop of a fragmented Đại Việt.

During his six-year tenure, Mạc Hiến Tông focused on strengthening the internal administration and military defenses of the Mạc heartland, primarily centered around Thăng Long (modern-day Hanoi). He continued the policies of his father, Mạc Thái Tông, and grandfather, Mạc Đăng Dung, in attempting to establish a stable bureaucracy and maintain the loyalty of the northern provinces. Despite the ongoing conflict, Mạc Hiến Tông's court engaged in diplomatic overtures, particularly with the Ming dynasty, to secure external recognition and bolster the Mạc's claim to the Mandate of Heaven, though these efforts yielded limited success against the backdrop of internal strife.

Mạc Hiến Tông's reign ensured the immediate continuity of the Mạc dynasty at a time when its very existence was under severe threat. His capacity to hold the line against the Lê-Trịnh forces, even temporarily, allowed the Mạc to persist as a significant political entity for several more decades. His death in 1546, leading to the succession of his younger brother Mạc Tuyên Tông, unfortunately coincided with an intensification of the civil war, plunging the dynasty into further instability and setting the stage for more decisive conflicts that would ultimately diminish Mạc power."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-06T23:16:54Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42309720987", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The establishment of the Mạc dynasty by his grandfather Mạc Đăng Dung in 1527 created a new imperial lineage in Đại Việt.", "His father, Mạc Thái Tông, died in 1540, necessitating his succession as the eldest son to the Mạc imperial throne.", "The ongoing civil war between the Mạc dynasty and the Restored Lê dynasty, supported by the Trịnh and Nguyễn clans, defined the political landscape he inherited."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-06T23:16:54Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42309720987", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1546-06-16"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1546-06-16"}], "newValue": ["His reign ensured the immediate continuity of the Mạc dynasty, maintaining its control over northern Đại Việt for another six years.", "The Mạc-Lê civil war continued unabated, further entr